## 309: Both requested methods

Distribution gives
x^3+10x^2+100x-10x^2-100x-1000 = x^3-1000.
The difference-of-cubes substitution a=x,b=10 gives the same polynomial.
Both methods are required by the source, not merely the final polynomial.

## 310: All six products to standard polynomial form

a) x^3-8.
b) p^3-125.
c) y^3+64.
d) 8a^3+1, because the cube base is 2a.
e) 1000x^3-27y^3, with bases 10x and 3y.
f) 64a^3+125b^3, with bases 4a and 5b.
Mixed terms cancel; the sign of the linear-factor second term selects sum/difference.

## 311: All twelve factorizations

a) (x-y)(x^2+xy+y^2).
b) (c-2)(c^2+2c+4).
c) (x+3)(x^2-3x+9).
d) (2x-3)(4x^2+6x+9).
e) (5a+1)(25a^2-5a+1).
f) (n-4m)(n^2+4nm+16m^2).
g) (y/2-10)(y^2/4+5y+100).
h) (x+1/3)(x^2-x/3+1/9).
i) (c-0.1)(c^2+0.1c+0.01).
j) (0.2b+5)(0.04b^2-b+25).
k) (0.4-ny)(0.16+0.4ny+n^2*y^2).
l) (4x+y/3)(16x^2-(4/3)xy+y^2/9).
These are polynomial identities, with rational/decimal cube roots exact.

## 312: Four divisibility proof certificates

a) 327^3+173^3 = (327+173)(327^2-327*173+173^2)
   = 500*80287. The second factor is an integer.
b) 731^3-611^3 = (731-611)(731^2+731*611+611^2)
   = 120*1354323.
c) 4759^3-2779^3 = (4759-2779)(4759^2+4759*2779+2779^2)
   = 1980*43596183.
d) 386^3+214^3 = 600*(386^2-386*214+214^2)
   = 600*112188 = 1200*56094.
   Merely exhibiting the factor 600 would not prove divisibility by 1200:
   the remaining factor is even (all three terms are even).

## 313: Both rational simplifications of radicals

a) Difference of cubes divided by 59-41=18 gives 59^2+59*41+41^2.
   Adding 59*41 yields (59+41)^2=10000. Principal square root = 100.
b) Sum of cubes divided by 67+52=119 gives 67^2-67*52+52^2.
   Subtracting 67*52 yields (67-52)^2=225. Principal square root = 15.
Using absolute values in sqrt(t^2)=|t| justifies both positive outputs.

## 314: Four reductions with original denominator restrictions

a) (y^3-1000)/(y-10) = y^2+10y+100, y != 10.
b) (8x^3+27)/(6x+9) = (4x^2-6x+9)/3, x != -3/2.
c) (a^2+6a+36)/(a^3-216) = 1/(a-6), a != 6.
d) (18b^2+24b+32)/(27b^3-64) = 2/(3b-4), b != 4/3.
The quadratic factors in c,d are positive for real variables. Cancellation
cannot extend the original domain to an excluded denominator zero.

## 315: Four combined fractions

Working over real variables and retaining original denominator restrictions:
a) Let Q=p^2+pq+q^2. The common numerator Q-3pq=(p-q)^2.
   Result (p-q)/Q, p != q. Q=0 only at p=q=0, already excluded.
b) Denominator (a+2)(a^2-2a+4), numerator
   6a^3+12a-3a^2(a+2)=3a(a^2-2a+4).
   Result 3a/(a+2), a != -2.
c) With Q=x^2+x+1, numerator
   2x^2+7x+3-(1-2x)(x-1)-3Q = Q.
   Result 1/(x-1), x != 1.
d) With Q=y^2+2y+4, numerator (y-2)^2-6y+Q=2(y-2)^2.
   Result 2(y-2)/Q, y != 2.
The quadratics in b,c,d have negative discriminants and positive leading terms.

## 316: All six products

a) (x^2+y^2)(x^4-x^2*y^2+y^4).
b) (a-b)(a+b)(a^2+ab+b^2)(a^2-ab+b^2).
c) (c-1)(c+1)(c^2+c+1)(c^2-c+1).
d) (n^2+1)(n^4-n^2+1).
e) (y-2)(y+2)(y^2+2y+4)(y^2-2y+4).
f) (2k-n)(2k+n)(4k^2+2kn+n^2)(4k^2-2kn+n^2).
Equally valid partial factorizations include (a^3-b^3)(a^3+b^3),
(c^3-1)(c^3+1), (y^3-8)(y^3+8), and
(8k^3-n^3)(8k^3+n^3). The source asks for a product, not necessarily an
irreducible real factorization. Sum-of-sixth-powers uses cubes of squares.

## 317: Four binomial-times-trinomial forms

a) (a+5)(a^2+13a+43).
b) (y+1)(y^2-7y+19).
c) (x-1)(x^2+10x+37).
d) (2x-y)(x^2-xy+y^2).
For d, the second cube base is x-y; expanding
x^2-x(x-y)+(x-y)^2 gives x^2-xy+y^2.

## 318: Three applications of identity (1)

a) x^6-1: six terms in the degree-five geometric sum, b=1,n=6.
b) y^4-16: b=2,n=4.
c) a^5-243: b=3,n=5.
These identities hold for all variable values, including equality of the bases.

## 319: Both reductions and restrictions

a) x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1, x != 1.
b) (y^6+y^5+y^4+y^3+y^2+y+1)/2, y != 1.
The extra factor 2 in 2y-2 must remain. The resulting polynomials have values
at 1, but the original fractions do not.

## 320: Both directed relations, using actual retained definitions

a) Yes. Domain is all points of A-B-C. Every point has exactly one abscissa.
   Distinct points sharing an abscissa do not violate input uniqueness.
   The input is a point, not its x-coordinate; general-set inputs are allowed
   by the definition paragraphs. Image in the numerical output set is [1,6].
b) No. Input is each real x in [1,6], output is a point on A-B-C with that x.
   On AB (1 <= x <= 3), y=(3/2)x+1/2.
   On BC (1 <= x <= 6), y=11/5-x/5.
   At x=2, there are distinct points (2,7/2) and (2,9/5).
   Every x in (1,3] has two possible points; x=1 has only B, and x>3
   has only its BC point. Thus the stipulated correspondence is not
   single-valued. No choice/selection rule was specified in the source.
The closed endpoints and direction distinguish the two tasks.

## 321: Whole parabola construction and closed-interval image

y=x^2-2x-15=(x-1)^2-16, upward-opening parabola with axis x=1,
vertex (1,-16), x-intercepts (-3,0),(5,0), y-intercept (0,-15).
Sample construction points:
x=-3,-1,0,1,2,3,5,6 yield y=0,-12,-15,-16,-15,-12,0,9.
The function decreases up to x=1 and increases after x=1.
For [-1,6], the minimum -16 occurs at x=1, endpoint values are
y(-1)=-12 and y(6)=9, so the maximum is 9 at x=6.
Continuity supplies every intermediate value: image [-16,9], endpoints included.
The source separately requires producing the graph, not just reporting this range.

## 322: Both rationality proofs

a) (5+2sqrt(6))^2 = 49+20sqrt(6);
   (5-2sqrt(6))^2 = 49-20sqrt(6). Sum = 98, an integer.
b) Both radicands are positive since 7^2 > (2sqrt(10))^2.
   Squaring the sum gives 14+2sqrt((7+2sqrt(10))(7-2sqrt(10)))
   =14+2sqrt(9)=20, an integer. Principal roots and positivity justify
   combining the radical product. Showing only a decimal approximation
   would not prove rationality.

